{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>He found him in a land of desert, etc.</b> This verse may be interpreted in praise of Israel — to say that even in a land of desert, a place of chaos, they went after Him, blessed be He, as it is written (Jeremiah 2:2): ״זָכַרְתִּי לָךְ חֶסֶד נְעוּרַיִךְ לֶכְתֵּךְ אַחֲרַי בַּמִּדְבָּר״ and so forth — according to the interpretation of Rashi. But according to the implication of the passage that comes to reprove them, as it is said (Deuteronomy 31:27): ״כִּי יָדַעְתִּי אֶת מֶרְיְךָ וְאֶת עָרְפְּךָ הַקָּשֶׁה״ and so forth, we may say that He speaks in their dispraise, for it is known that in the land of Egypt they were evil and very sinful toward HaShem, as is found in Ezekiel (20:5–7): ״וָאִוָּדַע לָהֶם בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ and so forth, ״וָאֹמַר אֲלֵהֶם אִישׁ שִׁקּוּצֵי עֵינָיו הַשְׁלִיכוּ״ and so forth, ״וְלֹא אָבוּ לִשְׁמֹעַ״ and so forth. And He juxtaposed this to the reproofs above and said: concerning all the distinctions that I granted you, in exaltation above all the peoples — nevertheless you did not cleave to Me, and even this, while you were in the iron crucible in the land of Egypt, those sufferings did not turn you back for the better — ״אָכְלָם בְּפִיהֶם״ (Psalms 78:30). But when He brought them out to the desert, a place of chaos, a howling wilderness, with no seed or fig, no bread to eat, no water to drink — then their uncircumcised hearts were humbled and they returned to HaShem. This is what it says: ״יִמְצָאֵהוּ בְּאֶרֶץ מִדְבָּר״ — not before then. For although they suffered afflictions in Egypt, HaShem did not find their heart whole with Him; only in the desert, because of the greatness of the pressure of hunger and thirst, then HaShem found their heart whole with Him; then ״יְסֹבְבֶנְהוּ״ in clouds of His glory; ״יְבוֹנְנֵהוּ״ with knowledge, understanding, and insight in the giving of the Torah; and ״יִצְּרֶנְהוּ כְּאִישׁוֹן עֵינוֹ״ — not the eye of flesh toward Him, blessed be He. And the Ramban explained that it refers to man, just as a person guards the pupil of his eye." "And by way of allusion it alludes to the Torah, which was written in black fire upon white fire — just as the eye has in it black and white, so the Torah has in it the revealed, resembling the white appearance, and the hidden, resembling the black appearance. Therefore ״יִצְּרֶנְהוּ״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, guarded them just as they guard the Torah, which is likened to the pupil of the eye, for these two forms of guarding depend one upon the other." "<b>As an eagle stirs up his nest, hovers over his fledglings.</b> Here the eagle is above and the fledglings below, and afterward: ״יִפְרֹשׂ כְּנָפָיו יִקָּחֵהוּ יִשָּׂאֵהוּ עַל אֶבְרָתוֹ״ — here the fledglings are above the eagle. This accords with the opinion of the Midrash (Song of Songs Rabbah 3:21) which the Ramban cited in parashat Chayei Sarah (Genesis 24:1), that Israel are called daughter and sister and mother, etc. For as it appears, this is not in the order of rungs. And it appears that at first the Holy One, blessed be He, called Israel daughter — ״שִׁמְעִי בַת״ and so forth (Psalms 45:11) — for just as a mother rules over the daughter, so the Holy One, blessed be He, rules over Israel. And afterward He called them ״אֲחוֹתִי,״ for every brother and sister are equal; thus the righteous are as though they became partners with the Holy One, blessed be He, etc. And afterward He called them ״אֵם,״ for just as the mother rules over the daughter, so a tzaddik rules over the fear of HaShem, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Makkot 16a): said the Holy One, blessed be He — who rules over Me? The tzaddik, etc. And we have already explained all this in parashat Yitro (Exodus 19:4) on the verse ״וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים״ and so forth. And if you have a difficulty, it is expounded there, the verse ״וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים״ at the time He calls them ״בַּת,״ as when a nurse carries the infant; and here perforce you must explain ״יִקָּחֵהוּ יִשָּׂאֵהוּ עַל אֶבְרָתוֹ״ to mean that a tzaddik rules over the fear of HaShem. The answer to this: there He mentioned the wings but not on His body, whereas here, in place of the wings, He mentioned ״אֶבְרָתוֹ.״ And I found explicitly written in the words of the grammarians that אֶבְרָה means the feathers upon His body literally, and this indeed indicates that the Holy One, blessed be He, granted the tzaddik authority over Him — that He, blessed be He, decrees a decree and the tzaddik annuls it. And these two extremes are mentioned here, and He omitted the middle term that is between these two extremes." "And if you wish to interpret ״יִפְרֹשׂ כְּנָפָיו יִקָּחֵהוּ״ — that is, to Himself and not upon Himself — all three rungs are alluded to here in their order: for first ״עַל גּוֹזָלָיו יְרַחֵף״ — the fledglings are below Him, blessed be He; this is when He called them ״בַּת.״ And afterward ״יִפְרֹשׂ כְּנָפָיו יִקָּחֵהוּ״ — to Himself, resembling Him; this is when He called them ״אֲחוֹתִי.״ And afterward ״יִשָּׂאֵהוּ עַל אֶבְרָתוֹ״ — the fledglings are above Him, blessed be He, as it were; this is when He called them ״אֵם,״ in the manner that these three rungs were mentioned in parashat Yitro. See there the entire matter and you will find it explained well."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The word המחברים (ha-mechabberim) literally means 'the authors/grammarians'; resolved as referring to lexicographers or grammarians who define Hebrew terms like אברה."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:14.962286532Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}